# Accessible Document Style

Write plain prose for later Grade 2 braille. Transcribe faithfully — do not change lesson content.

## Paragraphs and headings
Preserve the book's intended paragraphs. Do not merge unrelated blocks or split one thought across files.

Apply titles with heading levels that match the book's hierarchy.

BAD: `# Adjectives` or `## Tip` when the book uses a plain label
GOOD: a title-case or all-caps heading that matches the book, then body text
GOOD: `Tip:` `Note:` `FYI:` `Directions:` `Examples` `Caption:` on their own lines when the book prints them that way

## Numbers, dates, and phones
BAD: `1000 students` when the book shows a multi-digit quantity meant for reading aloud
GOOD: `1,000 students` when comma grouping aids comprehension
BAD: `March 4 2026` or `(555) 123-4567`
GOOD: `March-4-2026` or `555-123-4567` using `-` as the separator

## Lists and tables
Tables, numbered lists, lettered lists, and bullet points are allowed.

BAD: nested numbered lists (`1.` under `2.`)
BAD: nested lettered lists (`a.` under `b.`)
GOOD: flatten nested practice — renumber or reletter at one level only
GOOD: `• identify adjectives` or `1.` `2.` `3.` at a single level
GOOD: simple markdown tables when the book shows tabular data

## Transcriber notes
Use a separate paragraph to describe something visual on the page when it cannot be converted accessibly.

BAD: `[Photo of a dog]` when the caption already says `Caption: A golden retriever sits in grass.`
GOOD: convert the caption and skip the note when no extra value remains
GOOD: `Transcriber note: A color photograph shows three bar graphs without numeric labels.` when the lesson depends on unseen layout

## Symbols to avoid
Avoid `[]`, `#`, `&`, and `*` unless those characters appear explicitly in the source text.

BAD: `Adjectives & Articles` or `#1` or `*(required)*` or `**Tip:**`
GOOD: `Adjectives and Articles`
GOOD: write "and" not `&`
GOOD: a section break as `---`

Possessive apostrophes, hyphens, dashes, and quotation marks are allowed when the book uses them.

After writing, search the file for `[]`, `#`, `&`, and `*` and rephrase any hit unless the symbol is truly in the source.
